WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.639
The reason we consider Coraline to qualify
as a horror story on this channel has a lot

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:07.819
to do with horrifying nightmare creatures
like the Beldam and her empty, button eyed

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:08.819
underlings.

00:00:08.819 --> 00:00:12.631
It’s easy to overlook other supernatural
aspects of the story, like a talking cat and

00:00:12.631 --> 00:00:14.910
the mysterious trio of ghost children.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.660
These spirits were victims of the Other Mother
whose souls were captured and eaten away at

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.789
-- over the years, to the point that they
no longer had their own identities.

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:25.900
But before their untimely kidnappings, these
three children were individuals with their

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.140
own unique stories.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:32.130
To learn how their life experiences ultimately
helped Coraline, stick around to the end of

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.130
this video.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.143
This lesson is sponsored by Audible.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.220
Welcome to Horror History.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:45.190
The book and the movie adaptation of Coraline
are very similar, but the one big difference

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:49.080
is the creation of a character named Wybie
in the movie, whose great-aunt was a casualty

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.460
of The Other Mother and plays a part in the
story as the Sweet Ghost Girl.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.100
No such familial connection was present in
the book.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.850
Instead, author Neil Gaiman does leave us
some clues that can help us figure out what

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.830
these kids’ lives may have been like.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:04.280
For example, I researched the usage of words
in the ghost boy’s vocabulary and found

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.700
the only time period where each of these words
were popular in the lexicon was from about

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.280
1750 to 1850, also known as the Georgian Era
of Great Britain, so let’s take it back

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.910
a few centuries to the beginning of his story.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.609
The small ghost boy likely came from a wealthy
family who lived on the plot of land that

00:01:24.609 --> 00:01:28.259
would eventually become the location of apartments
where the Jones family lived.

00:01:28.259 --> 00:01:30.799
There are multiple times when the boy mentions
his governess.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.869
A governess is a woman employed to teach and
train school age children in a private household,

00:01:34.869 --> 00:01:38.689
they were usually hired by rich European families
and have become somewhat rare since World

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:39.689
War 1.

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:43.229
While the movie Coraline takes place in the
Pacific Northwest United States, the novella

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:46.939
is set in the United Kingdom -- Coraline’s
father takes a train to spend the day in London

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:47.939
in Chapter 3.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:51.679
The ghost boy’s family probably built and
owned the house where the story is set.

00:01:51.679 --> 00:01:55.369
By the time of Coraline’s era, it had been
split up into four separate flats that rented

00:01:55.369 --> 00:01:56.610
out as apartments.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.889
Mr. Bobo’s level three flat is actually
renovated from an attic.

00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:03.179
Since the ghost boy’s family is implied
to be very wealthy, it makes sense that they’d

00:02:03.179 --> 00:02:06.939
have this huge estate, so big that it had
to be split up once the family no longer lived

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:07.541
there.

00:02:07.541 --> 00:02:11.129
It seems that one of the boy’s hobbies was
hoop rolling, an English sport and children’s

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:14.319
game where the participant would try to use
a stick to keep a hoop or wheel upright and

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:15.319
perform tricks.

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:18.849
The image of the governess carrying his hoop
and stick ended up being one of the boy’s

00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:22.760
longest lasting memories, even after he had
almost entirely forgotten who he was.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.849
The Governess not only taught him the three
basic subjects of reading, writing and arithmetic,

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:28.540
but also seemed to impart life lessons.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.799
At one point, she told him: “nobody is ever
given more to shoulder than he or she can

00:02:32.799 --> 00:02:33.799
bear.”

00:02:33.799 --> 00:02:37.239
He would one pass this knowledge onto Coraline
so that she knew she wasn’t alone in her

00:02:37.239 --> 00:02:39.030
battle against the ancient creature.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.569
As a youngster, Ghost Boy had long curly hair
and wore breeches, a gown or dress-like article

00:02:43.569 --> 00:02:47.840
of clothing that young boys of about 2 to
8 years old were dressed in from the 1500s

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:48.870
to the early 1800s.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:53.120
Breeches were given as a right of passage,
most likely associated with toilet training.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.909
Maybe I need to get my cousin a pair of britches
before this Thanksgiving.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:58.450
The boy enjoyed eating cooked trout and boiled
potatoes.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:02.439
As he got older, his family required him to
have his curly hair cut short.

00:03:02.439 --> 00:03:06.650
He would be the earliest known target of the
evil, world-building entity referred to as

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:07.650
The Beldam.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.560
She used her skills as a seamstress to make
herself look like the boy’s mother.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.069
He briefly explains how she manipulated him.

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:18.209
“I walked through the scullery door … and
I found myself back in the parlor.

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:20.420
But she was waiting for me.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:25.049
She told me she was my other mamma, but I
never saw my true mamma again.”

00:03:25.049 --> 00:03:28.939
The boy’s soul was taken, and hidden within
a marble inside The Other World from which

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:30.049
the Beldam presides.

00:03:30.049 --> 00:03:33.690
The marble appears grey to most, but takes
on a fiery ember color when viewed through

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:37.300
a mystical adder stone -- the same color
as the tulips at his estate and the embers

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.670
of the fire that burned in his nursery on
cold winter evenings.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.569
Since they’ve all lost their individual
identities, it’s hard to tell who is who

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:47.420
when they’re speaking to Coraline, but using
clues in the prose and the vocabulary each

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:51.720
character uses, I was able to go through and
assign each line to one of the ghost children.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.719
We know the next one to be born is the girl
who wasn’t in the movie, because she at

00:03:54.719 --> 00:03:58.439
one point says, “this is the finest food
I’ve eaten in centuries,” implying that

00:03:58.439 --> 00:04:00.439
she’s at least a couple hundred years old.

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:04.420
So she’s probably the one using words like
“thou”, “aye” and “care’st”

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.340
meaning she was probably born in the 1860s
in Britain’s Victorian era.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.079
Not as much is known about this girl, but
she’s described as being a very pale child,

00:04:12.079 --> 00:04:15.690
dressed in what seemed to be spider’s webs,
with a circle of glittering silver set in

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:16.709
her blonde hair.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:20.390
We never learn her name, so I’m just gonna
call her the Goth Ghost Girl, which is ironically,

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:23.800
the same thing I call all of the girls that
follow me on Instagram.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.160
She seems to really like flowers, and she
possibly eats them.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:30.430
Coraline takes a great liking to her when
they do finally meet, but unfortunately for

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:34.960
her, she too was tricked and captured by The
Beldam -- dashing her opportunity to one day

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:35.960
grow up.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:39.919
She expresses that her joy was taken from
her, leaving her an empty husk with no heart

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:40.919
or soul.

00:04:40.919 --> 00:04:44.650
The last of the children to be born was The
Tall Ghost Girl, who uses mostly more contemporary

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.790
language with occasional exceptions.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:50.180
At the picnic in Coraline’s dream, she’s
eating sliced bread and jam.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.760
Sliced bread was invented in 1928, so I’ll
place her birth date in the 1930s.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.750
She wore a brown shapeless dress, and a brown
bonnet that was tied under her chin, matching

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:00.520
the color of her soul marble.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.570
She describes the Beldam feeding on her, until
there was nothing left.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:06.010
“Only snakeskins and spider husks.”

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:09.340
As the Beldam makes use of their souls for
sustenance, they begin to lose their unique

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.370
traits, and their memories fade away.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.979
Their names are the first to be forgotten,
and before long, they could not remember much

00:05:14.979 --> 00:05:19.160
of anything, including their own gender in
life, but they do know what the Beldam did

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.160
to them.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.490
Despite this, they do continue to exist in
ghost form.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:25.949
What I’m about to say isn’t confirmed,
but I do have a theory about what the Beldam

00:05:25.949 --> 00:05:27.210
does with their human bodies.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.370
It’s stated that “the Other Mother could
not create.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.520
She could only transform, and twist, and change”
things that already existed.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:37.240
Her world is inhabited by “Other” versions
of the people in Coraline’s world.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.139
These beings also serve as The Beldam’s
servants.

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:43.539
There are three other humanoid entities: the
Other Ms. Spink, the Other Ms. Forcible and

00:05:43.539 --> 00:05:44.539
The Other Father.

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:48.090
Remember, the Other crazy man upstairs is
really just a pack of rats in human clothing

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:49.750
and the Other Wybie is not in the book.

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:52.800
So we’re left with these three: two female
and one male.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.710
I think these creatures were created by The
Other Mother out of the deceased bodies of

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:57.920
the three ghost children.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.240
Another century goes by until Coraline becomes
the fourth known child lured into The Other

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.140
World by the Other Mother.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.160
As I mentioned near the beginning of this
video, the Neil Gaiman novella, Coraline,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.150
is the canon material that I use as my basis
when analysing a character’s history.

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:20.490
The graphic novel, the movie, the video game
-- these are all adaptations, they aren’t

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.520
created by the original author, so they aren’t
considered canon.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.550
But there is one more piece of content in
the Coraline canon.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:31.000
The audiobook, which is available on Audible,
is narrated by the original author, Neil Gaiman.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.750
The spoken words in the audiobook are the
same as the text in the novel, but this also

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:39.319
means the inflections, accents and enunciations
of the words can be analysed when trying to

00:06:39.319 --> 00:06:42.960
determine the author’s message; something
I will be doing in future videos.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.660
I use Audible as the definitive version of
the story.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:50.249
It even includes interludes with original
music, including the very creepy song of the rats.

00:06:50.249 --> 00:06:54.642
🎵 "We have teeth and we have tails.

00:06:54.642 --> 00:06:59.080
🎵 We have tails. We have eyes.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:03.906
🎵 We were here before you fell.

00:07:03.906 --> 00:07:08.120
🎵 You will be here when we rise..."

